### Method 2: Using Regular Expressions (`replace()`)

This method is more concise but can be slightly harder to read for beginners.

```javascript
/**
 * Capitalizes the first letter of each word using a regular expression.
 * @param {string} sentence The input string.
 * @returns {string} The sentence with each word capitalized.
 */
function capitalizeWordsRegex(sentence) {
  if (!sentence) {
    return "";
  }

  // The regex /\b\w/g matches:
  // \b : a word boundary (the start of a word)
  // \w : any word character (alphanumeric or underscore)
  // g : global flag (find all matches, not just the first one)
  return sentence.toLowerCase().replace(/\b\w/g, char => char.toUpperCase());
}

// --- Examples ---
console.log(capitalizeWordsRegex("this is another example")); // Output: This Is Another Example
console.log(capitalizeWordsRegex("another-test"));           // Output: Another-Test (Note: Hyphenated words are treated as one word)
```

